helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153255				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>GetProcessServiceManager function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>ProcessServiceManager\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153255				0	he	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03131700.xhp\" name=\"GetProcessServiceManager Function [Runtime]\"\>GetProcessServiceManager Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	par_id3156414				0	he	Returns the ProcessServiceManager (central Uno ServiceManager).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	par_id3145136				0	he	This function is required when you want to instantiate a service using CreateInstanceWithArguments.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153681				0	he	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	par_id3151110				0	he	oServiceManager = GetProcessServiceManager()				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149516				0	he	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	par_id3143270				0	he	oServiceManager = GetProcessServiceManager()				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	par_id3153825				0	he	oIntrospection = oServiceManager.createInstance("com.sun.star.beans.Introspection");				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	par_id3148473				0	he	this is the same as the following statement:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03131700.xhp	0	help	par_id3145609				0	he	oIntrospection = CreateUnoService("com.sun.star.beans.Introspection")				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	tit				0	he	Libraries, Modules and Dialogs				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147317				0	he	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/01020500.xhp\" name=\"Libraries, Modules and Dialogs\"\>Libraries, Modules and Dialogs\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3147427				0	he	The following describes the basic use of libraries, modules and dialogs in $[officename] Basic.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3146120				0	he	$[officename] Basic provides tools to help you structuring your projects. It supports various "units" which enable you to group individual SUBS and FUNCTIONS in a Basic project.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148575				0	he	Libraries				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3150011				0	he	Libraries serve as a tool for organizing modules, and can either be attached to a document or a template. When the document or a template is saved, all modules contained in the library are automatically saved as well.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3151112				0	he	A library can contain up to 16,000 modules.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149262				0	he	Modules				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3156441				0	he	A module contains SUBS and FUNCTIONS along with variable declarations. The length of the program that can be saved in a module is limited to 64 KB. If more space is required you can divide a $[officename] Basic project among several modules, and then save them in a single library.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152577				0	he	Dialog Modules				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\01020500.xhp	0	help	par_id3149377				0	he	Dialog modules contain dialog definitions, including the dialog box properties, the properties of each dialog element and the events assigned. Since a dialog module can only contain a single dialog, they are often referred to as "dialogs".				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000003.xhp	0	help	tit				0	he	Information				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000003.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148550				0	he	Information				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_id3153381				0	he	You can set the locale used for controlling the formatting numbers, dates and currencies in $[officename] Basic in \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Language Settings - Languages\</emph\>. In Basic format codes, the decimal point (\<emph\>.\</emph\>) is always used as \<emph\>placeholder\</emph\> for the decimal separator defined in your locale and will be replaced by the corresponding character.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\00000003.xhp	0	help	par_id3150870				0	he	The same applies to the locale settings for date, time and currency formats. The Basic format code will be interpreted and displayed according to your locale setting.				20200411 15:09:53
