helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3154908				0	he	Date				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154124				0	he	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153193				0	he	\<emph\>hour:\</emph\> Any integer expression that indicates the hour of the time that is used to determine the serial time value. Valid values: 0-23.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252				0	he	\<emph\>minute:\</emph\> Any integer expression that indicates the minute of the time that is used to determine the serial time value. In general, use values between 0 and 59. However, you can also use values that lie outside of this range, where the number of minutes influence the hour value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3161831				0	he	\<emph\>second:\</emph\> Any integer expression that indicates the second of the time that is used to determine the serial time value. In general, you can use values between 0 and 59. However, you can also use values that lie outside of this range, where the number seconds influences the minute value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3155854				0	he	\<emph\>Examples:\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153952				0	he	12, -5, 45 corresponds to 11, 55, 45				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3147349				0	he	12, 61, 45 corresponds to 13, 2, 45				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3147426				0	he	12, 20, -2 corresponds to 12, 19, 58				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153365				0	he	12, 20, 63 corresponds to 12, 21, 4				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3146985				0	he	You can use the TimeSerial function to convert any time into a single value that you can use to calculate time differences.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3155308				0	he	The TimeSerial function returns the type Variant with VarType 7 (Date). This value is stored internally as a double-precision number between 0 and 0.9999999999. As opposed to the DateSerial or DateValue function, where the serial date values are calculated as days relative to a fixed date, you can calculate with values returned by the TimeSerial function, but you cannot evaluate them.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3149482				0	he	In the TimeValue function, you can pass a string as a parameter containing the time. For the TimeSerial function, however, you can pass the individual parameters (hour, minute, second) as separate numeric expressions.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154790				0	he	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3145252				0	he	Sub ExampleTimeSerial				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153157				0	he	Dim dDate As Double, sDate As String				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3156286				0	he	dDate = TimeSerial(8,30,15)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3148456				0	he	sDate = TimeSerial(8,30,15)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3155600				0	he	MsgBox dDate,64,"Time as a number"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153417				0	he	MsgBox sDate,64,"Formatted time"				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03030205.xhp	0	help	par_id3153836				0	he	End Sub				20200411 15:09:53
