helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	tit				0	he	Format Function [Runtime]				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	bm_id3153539				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>Format function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153539				0	he	\<link href=\"text/sbasic/shared/03120301.xhp\" name=\"Format Function [Runtime]\"\>Format Function [Runtime]\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156042				0	he	Converts a number to a string, and then formats it according to the format that you specify.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145090				0	he	Syntax:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153527				0	he	Format (Number [, Format As String])				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149178				0	he	Return value:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148474				0	he	String				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159176				0	he	Parameters:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149415				0	he	\<emph\>Number:\</emph\> Numeric expression that you want to convert to a formatted string.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147531				0	he	\<emph\>Format:\</emph\> String that specifies the format code for the number. If \<emph\>Format\</emph\> is omitted, the Format function works like the \<emph\>Str\</emph\> function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147561				0	he	Formatting Codes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147265				0	he	The following list describes the codes that you can use for formatting a number:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153380				0	he	\<emph\>0:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has a digit at the position of the 0 in the format code, the digit is displayed, otherwise a zero is displayed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151210				0	he	If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> has fewer digits than the number of zeros in the format code, (on either side of the decimal), leading or trailing zeros are displayed. If the number has more digits to the left of the decimal separator than the amount of zeros in the format code, the additional digits are displayed without formatting.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3151176	14			0	he	Decimal places in the number are rounded according to the number of zeros that appear after the decimal separator in the \<emph\>Format \</emph\>code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154123				0	he	\<emph\>#:\</emph\> If \<emph\>Number\</emph\> contains a digit at the position of the # placeholder in the \<emph\>Format\</emph\> code, the digit is displayed, otherwise nothing is displayed at this position.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148452	16			0	he	This symbol works like the 0, except that leading or trailing zeroes are not displayed if there are more # characters in the format code than digits in the number. Only the relevant digits of the number are displayed.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159150				0	he	\<emph\>.:\</emph\> The decimal placeholder determines the number of decimal places to the left and right of the decimal separator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3159252				0	he	If the format code contains only # placeholders to the left of this symbol, numbers less than 1 begin with a decimal separator. To always display a leading zero with fractional numbers, use 0 as a placeholder for the first digit to the left of the decimal separator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153368				0	he	\<emph\>%:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and inserts the percent sign (%) where the number appears in the format code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149481				0	he	\<emph\>E- E+ e- e+ :\</emph\> If the format code contains at least one digit placeholder (0 or #) to the right of the symbol E-, E+, e-, or e+, the number is formatted in the scientific or exponential format. The letter E or e is inserted between the number and the exponent. The number of placeholders for digits to the right of the symbol determines the number of digits in the exponent.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3149262				0	he	If the exponent is negative, a minus sign is displayed directly before an exponent with E-, E+, e-, e+. If the exponent is positive, a plus sign is only displayed before exponents with E+ or e+.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148617				0	he	The thousands delimiter is displayed if the format code contains the delimiter enclosed by digit placeholders (0 or #).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3163713				0	he	The use of a period as a thousands and decimal separator is dependent on the regional setting. When you enter a number directly in Basic source code, always use a period as decimal delimiter. The actual character displayed as a decimal separator depends on the number format in your system settings.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3152887				0	he	\<emph\>- + $ ( ) space:\</emph\> A plus (+), minus (-), dollar ($), space, or brackets entered directly in the format code is displayed as a literal character.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148576				0	he	To display characters other than the ones listed here, you must precede it by a backslash (\\), or enclose it in quotation marks (" ").				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153139				0	he	\\ : The backslash displays the next character in the format code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153366				0	he	Characters in the format code that have a special meaning can only be displayed as literal characters if they are preceded by a backslash. The backslash itself is not displayed, unless you enter a double backslash (\\\\) in the format code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3155411				0	he	Characters that must be preceded by a backslash in the format code in order to be displayed as literal characters are date- and time-formatting characters (a, c, d, h, m, n, p, q, s, t, w, y, /, :), numeric-formatting characters (#, 0, %, E, e, comma, period), and string-formatting characters (@, &, <, >, !).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3145749				0	he	You can also use the following predefined number formats. Except for "General Number", all of the predefined format codes return the number as a decimal number with two decimal places.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150113				0	he	If you use predefined formats, the name of the format must be enclosed in quotation marks.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149377				0	he	Predefined format				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154730				0	he	\<emph\>General Number:\</emph\> Numbers are displayed as entered.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153158				0	he	\<emph\>Currency:\</emph\> Inserts a dollar sign in front of the number and encloses negative numbers in brackets.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3154490				0	he	\<emph\>Fixed:\</emph\> Displays at least one digit in front of the decimal separator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153415				0	he	\<emph\>Standard:\</emph\> Displays numbers with a thousands separator.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3150715				0	he	\<emph\>Percent:\</emph\> Multiplies the number by 100 and appends a percent sign to the number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153836				0	he	\<emph\>Scientific:\</emph\> Displays numbers in scientific format (for example, 1.00E+03 for 1000).				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3153707				0	he	A format code can be divided into three sections that are separated by semicolons. The first part defines the format for positive values, the second part for negative values, and the third part for zero. If you only specify one format code, it applies to all numbers.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	hd_id3149019				0	he	Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3156054				0	he	Sub ExampleFormat				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3148993				0	he	MsgBox Format(6328.2, "##,##0.00")				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_idN107A2				0	he	REM always use a period as decimal delimiter when you enter numbers in Basic source code.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\sbasic\shared\03120301.xhp	0	help	par_id3147339				0	he	REM displays for example 6,328.20 in English locale, 6.328,20 in German locale.				20200411 15:09:53
