helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	tit				0	he	Animating Objects in Presentation Slides				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	bm_id3150251				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>objects; moving along paths\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>connecting; paths and objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>paths; moving objects along\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>motion paths\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>applying; animation effects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>removing;animation effects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>effects;applying to/removing from objects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>adding;animation effects\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>animations;editing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>custom animation\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150251	31			0	he	\<variable id=\"animated_objects\"\>\<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/animated_objects.xhp\" name=\"Animating Objects in Slides\"\>Animating Objects in Presentation Slides\</link\> \</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id3150214	32			0	he	You can apply preset animation effects to objects on your slide. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154762	70			0	he	To apply an animation effect to an object: 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id3146964				0	he	On a slide in \<emph\>Normal\</emph\> view, select the object you want to animate.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875				0	he	Choose \<emph\>Slide Show - Custom Animation\</emph\>, click \<emph\>Add\</emph\>, and then select an animation effect.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id3166462				0	he	On the \<emph\>Add\</emph\> dialog, click a tab page to choose from a category of effects. Click an effect, then click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id3156060				0	he	To preview the animation, click the \<emph\>Play\</emph\> button.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	hd_id2476577				0	he	To apply and edit a motion path effect:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id4217047				0	he	An object can be animated to move along a motion path. You can use predefined or your own motion paths.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id2629474				0	he	If you select "Curve", "Polygon", or "Freeform Line", the dialog closes and you can draw your own path. If the drawing is finished and not canceled, the created path is removed from the document and inserted as a motion path effect.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id8069704				0	he	 \<emph\>Editing motion paths\</emph\> 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id4524674				0	he	If the Custom Animation Panel is visible, the motion paths of all effects of the current slide are drawn as a transparent overlay on the slide. All paths are visible all the time, therefore animations with consecutive paths can be created easily. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id4396801				0	he	A motion path can be selected by clicking on the path. A selected path will support handles, it can be moved and resized like a shape. A double click on a path starts the point edit mode. The point edit mode can also be started by \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>Edit - Points\</item\> or by pressing \<item type=\"keycode\"\>F8\</item\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148387				0	he	To remove an animation effect from an object:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id3148774				0	he	On a slide in \<emph\>Normal\</emph\> view, select the object from which to remove the effect.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id3155372				0	he	Choose \<emph\>Slide Show - Custom Animation\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\animated_objects.xhp	0	help	par_id3153718				0	he	Click \<emph\>Remove\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	tit				0	he	Showing a Slide Show				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	bm_id5592516				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>running;slide shows\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>slide shows;showing\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>showing;slide shows\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	hd_id4901272				0	he	\<variable id=\"show\"\>\<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/show.xhp\" name=\"Showing a Slide Show\"\>Showing a Slide Show\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id9636829				0	he	Different ways exist to start a slide show. Once a slide show is running, you can take control pressing keys or clicking the mouse buttons.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id7076937				0	he	By default, a slide show always starts with the first slide. You advance manually through slides up to the last slide. You can change these settings.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	hd_id9954527				0	he	Running a Slide Show				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id6860255				0	he	Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>F5\</item\> to run the show.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id4199957				0	he	If you want all shows to start from the current slide instead of the first slide, choose \<emph\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - %PRODUCTNAME Impress - General\</emph\> and click \<emph\>Always with current page\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id3209936				0	he	Click to advance to the next effect or to the next slide.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id8702658				0	he	Press \<item type=\"keycode\"\>Esc\</item\> to abort the show before the end.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id3741264				0	he	Many more keys are available to \<link href=\"text/simpress/04/01020000.xhp\"\>control a slide show\</link\>. You can also right-click to open a context menu with useful commands.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	hd_id5694143				0	he	Showing an automatic slide show (kiosk mode)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id5829645				0	he	For an automatic change to the next slide, you must assign a slide transition to each slide. 				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id2361522				0	he	On the Task Pane, click \<emph\>Slide Transition\</emph\> to open that tab page.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id9168980				0	he	In the \<emph\>Advance slide\</emph\> area, click \<emph\>Automatically after\</emph\>, and select a time duration.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id9766533				0	he	Click \<emph\>Apply to All Slides\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id6081728				0	he	You can assign a different time for every slide to advance to the next slide. The \<link href=\"text/simpress/guide/rehearse_timings.xhp\"\>rehearse timings\</link\> feature can assist you to get the timing right.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id9219641				0	he	To advance to the first slide, after all slides have been shown, you must set the slide show to repeat automatically.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id3174230				0	he	Choose \<emph\>Slide Show - Slide Show Settings\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id1336405				0	he	In the Type area, click \<emph\>Auto\</emph\> and select a pause time between shows.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id4959257				0	he	When you create a new slide show using the \<link href=\"text/shared/autopi/01050000.xhp\"\>Presentation Wizard\</link\>, you can select the duration of slides and of the pause, on the third wizard page.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	hd_id1906491				0	he	Running a slide show from a file				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id4799340				0	he	You can start %PRODUCTNAME from a command prompt, followed by the parameter \<item type=\"literal\"\>-show\</item\> and an Impress filename. For example, to start the file \<item type=\"literal\"\>filename.odp\</item\> from the command prompt, enter the following command:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id1502121				0	he	\<item type=\"literal\"\>soffice -show filename.odp\</item\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\show.xhp	0	help	par_id8266853				0	he	This assumes that soffice is in the program path of your system, and that \<item type=\"literal\"\>filename.odp\</item\> is located in the current directory.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\simpress\guide\line_edit.xhp	0	help	tit				0	he	Editing Curves				20200411 15:09:53
