helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3144687				0	he	Column A contains several X1 values, column B several X2 values and column C the Y values. You have already entered these values in your spreadsheet. You have now set up E2:G6 in the spreadsheet and activated the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>. For the LINEST function to work, you must have marked the \<emph\>Array\</emph\> check box in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>. Next, select the following values in the spreadsheet (or enter them using the keyboard):				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158020				0	he	\<emph\>Data_Y\</emph\> is C2:C8				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158039				0	he	\<emph\>Data_X\</emph\> is A2:B8				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158058				0	he	\<emph\>Linear_Type\</emph\> and \<emph\>Stats\</emph\> are both set to 1.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158084				0	he	As soon as you click \<emph\>OK\</emph\>, $[officename] Calc will fill the above example with the LINEST values as shown in the example.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158106				0	he	The formula in the \<emph\>Formula\</emph\> Bar corresponds to each cell of the LINEST array {=LINEST(C2:C8;A2:B8;1;1)}				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158128				0	he	\<emph\>This represents the calculated LINEST values:\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158146				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>slopes, see also regression lines\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>regression lines\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158146				0	he	E2 and F2: Slope \<emph\>m\</emph\> of the regression line y=b+m*x for the x1 and x2 values. The values are given in reverse order; that is, the slope for x2 in E2 and the slope for x1 in F2.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158184				0	he	G2: Intersection b with the y axis.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3158204				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>standard errors\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3158204				0	he	E3 and F3: The standard error of the slope value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145845				0	he	G3: The standard error of the intercept				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145859				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>RSQ calculations\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145859				0	he	E4: RSQ				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145880				0	he	F4: The standard error of the regression calculated for the Y value.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145894				0	he	E5: The F value from the variance analysis.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145915				0	he	F5: The degrees of freedom from the variance analysis.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145937				0	he	E6: The sum of the squared deviation of the estimated Y values from their linear mean.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3145952				0	he	F6: The sum of the squared deviation of the estimated Y value from the given Y values.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11B04				0	he	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	bm_id1596728				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>LOGEST function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146009				0	he	LOGEST				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3146037				0	he	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_RKP\"\>This function calculates the adjustment of the entered data as an exponential regression curve (y=b*m^x).\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146056				0	he	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163123				0	he	LOGEST(Data_Y; Data_X; Function_Type; Stats)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163137				0	he	\<emph\>Data_Y\</emph\> represents the Y Data array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163155				0	he	\<emph\>Data_X\</emph\> (optional) represents the X Data array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163174				0	he	\<emph\>Function_Type\</emph\> (optional). If Function_Type = 0, functions in the form y = m^x will be calculated. Otherwise, y = b*m^x functions will be calculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_id3163196				0	he	\<emph\>Stats\</emph\> (optional). If Stats=0, only the regression coefficient is calculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN118F7				0	he	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#optional\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060107.xhp	0	help	par_idN11BC3				0	he	\<embedvar href=\"text/scalc/00/00000004.xhp#moreontop\"/\>				20200411 15:09:53
