helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06070000.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145673				0	he	\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/06070000.xhp\" name=\"AutoCalculate\"\>AutoCalculate\</link\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3148798				0	he	\<ahelp hid=\".uno:AutomaticCalculation\"\>Automatically recalculates all formulas in the document.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\06070000.xhp	0	help	par_id3145173				0	he	All cells are recalculated after a sheet cell has been modified. Any charts in the sheet will also be refreshed. If the\<emph\> AutoCalculate\</emph\> function is activated, the \<emph\>Recalculate\</emph\> function F9 is not available.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	tit				0	he	DATE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155511				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>DATE function\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	hd_id3155511				0	he	\<variable id=\"date\"\>\<link href=\"text/scalc/01/func_date.xhp\"\>DATE\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3153551				0	he	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_DATUM\"\>This function calculates a date specified by year, month, day and displays it in the cell's formatting.\</ahelp\> The default format of a cell containing the DATE function is the date format, but you can format the cells with any other number format.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148590				0	he	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3150474				0	he	DATE(year; month; day)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3152815				0	he	\<emph\>Year\</emph\> is an integer between 1583 and 9956 or 0 and 99.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3153222				0	he	In \<item type=\"menuitem\"\>\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - $[officename] - General \</item\>you can set from which year a two-digit number entry is recognized as 20xx.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3155817				0	he	\<emph\>Month\</emph\> is an integer between 1 and 12 indicating the month.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3153183				0	he	\<emph\>Day\</emph\> is a number between 1 and 31 indicating the day of the month.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3156260				0	he	If the values for month and day are higher, they are carried over to the next digit. If you enter =DATE(00;12;31) the result will be "12/31/00." If, on the other hand, you enter =DATE(00;13;31) the result will be "1/31/01."				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147477				0	he	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\func_date.xhp	0	help	par_id3152589				0	he	DATE("00;1;1") yields 1/1/00				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	tit				0	he	Consolidate by				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151210				0	he	Consolidate by				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3125864				0	he	Consolidate by				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3154909				0	he	Use this section if the cell ranges that you want to consolidate contain labels. You only need to select these options if the consolidation ranges contain similar labels and the data arranged is arranged differently.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153968				0	he	Row labels				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3150441				0	he	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_CONSOLIDATE:BTN_BYROW\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Uses the row labels to arrange the consolidated data.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146976				0	he	Column labels				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3155411				0	he	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_CONSOLIDATE:BTN_BYCOL\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Uses the column labels to arrange the consolidated data.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3153191				0	he	Options				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3159154				0	he	Link to source data				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3146986				0	he	\<ahelp hid=\"SC:CHECKBOX:RID_SCDLG_CONSOLIDATE:BTN_REFS\" visibility=\"visible\"\>Links the data in the consolidation range to the source data, and automatically updates the results of the consolidation when the source data is changed.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	hd_id3163708				0	he	More <<				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\12070100.xhp	0	help	par_id3151118				0	he	Hides the additional options.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	tit				0	he	Add-in for Programming in $[officename] Calc				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	bm_id3151076				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>programming; add-ins\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>shared libraries; programming\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>external DLL functions\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>functions; $[officename] Calc add-in DLL\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>add-ins; for programming\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3151076				0	he	Add-in for Programming in $[officename] Calc				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3147001				0	he	The method of extending Calc by Add-Ins that is described in the following is outdated. The interfaces are still valid and supported, to ensure compatibility with existing Add-Ins, but for programming new Add-Ins you should use the new \<link href=\"text/shared/guide/integratinguno.xhp\" name=\"API functions\"\>API functions\</link\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3150361	2			0	he	$[officename] Calc can be expanded by Add-Ins, which are external programming modules providing additional functions for working with spreadsheets. These are listed in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\> in the \<emph\>Add-In\</emph\> category. If you would like to program an Add-In yourself, you can learn here which functions must be exported by the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>shared library \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>external DLL\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> so that the Add-In can be successfully attached.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149211	3			0	he	$[officename] searches the Add-in folder defined in the configuration for a suitable \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>shared library \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>DLL\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>. To be recognized by $[officename], the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>shared library \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>DLL\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> must have certain properties, as explained in the following. This information allows you to program your own Add-In for \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\> of $[officename] Calc.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3146981				0	he	The Add-In Concept				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3156292				0	he	Each Add-In library provides several functions. Some functions are used for administrative purposes. You can choose almost any name for your own functions. However, they must also follow certain rules regarding parameter passing. The exact naming and calling conventions vary for different platforms.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3152890	6			0	he	Functions of \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>Shared Library \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>AddIn DLL\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3148837				0	he	At a minimum, the administrative functions \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060112.xhp\" name=\"GetFunctionCount\"\>GetFunctionCount\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060112.xhp\" name=\"GetFunctionData\"\>GetFunctionData\</link\> must exist. Using these, the functions as well as parameter types and return values can be determined. As return values, the Double and String types are supported. As parameters, additionally the cell areas \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060112.xhp\" name=\"Double Array\"\>Double Array\</link\>, \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060112.xhp\" name=\"String Array\"\>String Array\</link\>, and \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060112.xhp\" name=\"Cell Array\"\>Cell Array\</link\> are supported.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3148604				0	he	Parameters are passed using references. Therefore, a change of these values is basically possible. However, this is not supported in $[officename] Calc because it does not make sense within spreadsheets.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3150112				0	he	Libraries can be reloaded during runtime and their contents can be analyzed by the administrative functions. For each function, information is available about count and type of parameters, internal and external function names and an administrative number.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3155269				0	he	The functions are called synchronously and return their results immediately. Real time functions (asynchronous functions) are also possible; however, they are not explained in detail because of their complexity.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145077				0	he	General information about the interface				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3146776				0	he	The maximum number of parameters in an Add-In function attached to $[officename] Calc is 16: one return value and a maximum of 15 function input parameters.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149899				0	he	The data types are defined as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3151302				0	he	\<emph\>Data types\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3143222				0	he	\<emph\>Definition\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149384				0	he	CALLTYPE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3146963				0	he	Under Windows: FAR PASCAL (_far _pascal)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3153809				0	he	Other: default (operating system specific default)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3154734				0	he	USHORT				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3155760				0	he	2 Byte unsigned Integer				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3145320				0	he	DOUBLE				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3150956				0	he	8 byte platform-dependent format				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3146097				0	he	Paramtype				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3150432				0	he	Platform-dependent like int				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3153955				0	he	PTR_DOUBLE =0 pointer to a double				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3159262				0	he	PTR_STRING =1 pointer to a zero-terminated string				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3148747				0	he	PTR_DOUBLE_ARR =2 pointer to a double array				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3147406				0	he	PTR_STRING_ARR =3 pointer to a string array				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3151392				0	he	PTR_CELL_ARR =4 pointer to a cell array				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3153028				0	he	NONE =5				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156396	31			0	he	\<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>Shared Library \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>DLL\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> functions				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3153019	32			0	he	Following you will find a description of those functions, which are called at the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>Shared Library \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>external DLL\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3150038	33			0	he	For all \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>Shared Library \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>DLL\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> functions, the following applies:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3157876				0	he	void CALLTYPE fn(out, in1, in2, ...)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3147616				0	he	Output: Resulting value				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3159119				0	he	Input: Any number of types (double&, char*, double*, char**, Cell area), where the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060112.xhp\" name=\"Cell area\"\>Cell area\</link\> is an array of types double array, string array, or cell array.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150653				0	he	GetFunctionCount()				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3152981				0	he	Returns the number of functions without the management functions of the reference parameter. Each function has a unique number between 0 and nCount-1. This number will be needed for the \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060112.xhp\" name=\"GetFunctionData\"\>GetFunctionData\</link\> and \<link href=\"text/scalc/01/04060112.xhp\" name=\"GetParameterDescription\"\>GetParameterDescription\</link\> functions later.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3150742				0	he	\<emph\>Syntax\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3148728				0	he	void CALLTYPE GetFunctionCount(USHORT& nCount)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3154677				0	he	\<emph\>Parameter\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3146940				0	he	USHORT &nCount:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149893				0	he	Output: Reference to a variable, which is supposed to contain the number of Add-In functions. For example: If the Add-In provides 5 functions for $[officename] Calc, then nCount=5.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147476				0	he	GetFunctionData()				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3154841				0	he	Determines all the important information about an Add-In function.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3148888				0	he	\<emph\>Syntax\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3148434				0	he	void CALLTYPE GetFunctionData(USHORT& nNo, char* pFuncName, USHORT& nParamCount, Paramtype* peType, char* pInternalName)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149253				0	he	\<emph\>Parameter\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149686				0	he	USHORT& nNo:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149949				0	he	Input: Function number between 0 and nCount-1, inclusively.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149546				0	he	char* pFuncName:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3148579	52			0	he	Output: Function name as seen by the programmer, as it is named in the \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"UNIX\"\>Shared Library \</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>DLL\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\>. This name does not determine the name used in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3153935				0	he	USHORT& nParamCount:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3150142				0	he	Output: Number of parameters in AddIn function. This number must be greater than 0, because there is always a result value; the maximum value is 16.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3145143				0	he	Paramtype* peType:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3148750				0	he	Output: Pointer to an array of exactly 16 variables of type Paramtype. The first nParamCount entries are filled with the suitable type of parameter.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3153078				0	he	char* pInternalName:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3155261				0	he	Output: Function name as seen by the user, as it appears in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>. May contain umlauts.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3153327				0	he	The pFuncName and pInternalName parameters are char arrays, which are implemented with size 256 in $[officename] Calc.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148567				0	he	GetParameterDescription()				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3153000				0	he	Provides a brief description of the Add-In function and its parameters. As an option, this function can be used to show a function and parameter description in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3154501				0	he	\<emph\>Syntax\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3153564				0	he	void CALLTYPE GetParameterDescription(USHORT& nNo, USHORT& nParam, char* pName, char* pDesc)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3157995				0	he	\<emph\>Parameter\</emph\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3155925				0	he	USHORT& nNo:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149883				0	he	Input: Number of the function in the library; between 0 and nCount-1.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3154326				0	he	USHORT& nParam:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3159139				0	he	Input: Indicates, for which parameter the description is provided; parameters start at 1. If nParam is 0, the description itself is supposed to be provided in pDesc; in this case, pName does not have any meaning.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3147374				0	he	char* pName:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3145245				0	he	Output: Takes up the parameter name or type, for example, the word "Number" or "String" or "Date", and so on. Implemented in $[officename] Calc as char[256].				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3151020				0	he	char* pDesc:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3148389				0	he	Output: Takes up the description of the parameter, for example, "Value, at which the universe is to be calculated." Implemented in $[officename] Calc as char[256].				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3145303				0	he	pName and pDesc are char arrays; implemented in $[officename] Calc with size 256. Please note that the space available in the \<emph\>Function Wizard\</emph\> is limited and that the 256 characters cannot be fully used.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3148874				0	he	Cell areas				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3150265				0	he	The following tables contain information about which data structures must be provided by an external program module in order to pass cell areas. $[officename] Calc distinguishes between three different arrays, depending on the data type.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	hd_id3156060				0	he	Double Array				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060112.xhp	0	help	par_id3149540				0	he	As a parameter, a cell area with values of the Number/Double type can be passed. A double array in $[officename] Calc is defined as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
