helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	bm_id3147356				0	he	\<bookmark_value\>GCD function\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>greatest common divisor\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147356				0	he	GCD				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3152465				0	he	\<ahelp hid=\"HID_FUNC_GGT\"\>Returns the greatest common divisor of two or more integers.\</ahelp\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id2769249				0	he	The greatest common divisor is the positive largest integer which will divide, without remainder, each of the given integers.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3150643				0	he	Syntax				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3154524				0	he	GCD(integer 1 to 30)				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3149340				0	he	\<emph\>Integer 1 to 30\</emph\> are up to 30 integers whose greatest common divisor is to be calculated.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	hd_id3147317				0	he	Example				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id3151285				0	he	GCD(16;32;24) gives the result 8, because 8 is the largest number that can divide 16, 24 and 32 without a remainder.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\scalc\01\04060106.xhp	0	help	par_id1604663				0	he	GCD(B1:B3) where cells B1, B2, B3 contain 9, 12, 9 gives 3.				20200411 15:09:53
